# Docker build context exclusions.
# The builder compiles from cmd/ and internal/ only; everything below is either a
# secret, a host artefact, or documentation that would bloat the context and bust
# the layer cache on every commit.

# Git metadata — large, and changes on every commit
.git
.gitignore

# Environment files. dev/.env carries a real POSTGRES_PASSWORD today and .env.prod
# carries the production one on the deployment host. Neither may reach a build layer
# or a distributable image (SEC-001).
#
# Both anchored and recursive forms are listed on purpose: unlike .gitignore, a bare
# pattern in .dockerignore matches ONLY at the context root — it does not match at any
# depth. QA proved this on Story 3.1 by planting six decoys: the three root-level ones
# were excluded and internal/.env, cmd/secrets.env and internal/config/.env.prod all
# entered the build context. The ** forms below close that gap. (FIND-022)
.env
.env.*
*.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env

# Development, test and production stacks — none is needed to compile. The builder
# takes go.mod, go.sum, cmd/ and internal/ and nothing else; dev/ additionally holds
# the .env excluded above, and prod/ is created by this story.
dev/
test/
prod/

# Documentation and process artefacts — not needed to compile
docs/
.bmad-core/
.claude/
.vscode/
README.md
CLAUDE.md

# Host build and debug artefacts — the image builds its own binary from source
bin/
__debug_bin*
*.exe
*.test
*.out
.idea/
*.pid

# Production orchestration — consumed by Docker Compose, not by the build
docker-compose.prod.yml
